  "dom_id": "step-q87387",
  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "If $P(x)={x}^{4}-{x}^{2},$ solve $P(x)\\ge 0.$",
  "worked_solution": "To solve the inequality $P(x)\\ge 0,$ we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=P(x)$ lies above the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nFirst, we factor the polynomial $P(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & ={x}^{4}-{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}-1) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x+1)(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the polynomial $P(x)={x}^{4}-{x}^{2}$ are $x=-1,$ $x=0$ and $x=1,$  where\n\n- $x=0$ has multiplicity $2,$ and\n\n- $x=-1$ and $x=1$ are simple roots.\n\nSo, we can graph the curve as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/assets/bd322670886e03400e0197b93d931445e302f00a6a054fa8feddd9cb05d11d77.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving $P(x)\\ge 0,$ we look for the part of the curve that is above the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup {0}\\cup [1,\\infty ).$",
